  "analysis": {
    "why_you_should_care": "Storage engines are where 'it works on my SSD' becomes a research problem.",
    "what_happened": "An open-source embedded database published a readable implementation of paging, a write-ahead log, and recovery.",
    "how_it_works": "Pages on disk, a buffer pool in memory, a WAL for durability, checkpoints to bound recovery. Queries are extra credit; the engine is the story.",
    "under_the_hood": "Look at page headers, endianness, checksums, and how they decide a transaction is durable. If they skip fsync, that is also a finding.",
    "why_they_built_it": "To understand the machine that SQLite made look easy.",
    "what_was_difficult": "Crash consistency. Everything else is vocabulary.",
    "performance": "The available sources do not establish independent numbers.",
    "trade_offs": "Simplicity versus concurrent writers. Educational clarity versus LSM fashion.",
    "what_is_surprising": "The WAL is short. The bugs it prevents are not.",
    "quick_read": "A from-scratch database whose pages you can actually read.",
